<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The physics experiment system is served over plain HTTP, and only that system is.

  `wlsy.xidian.edu.cn` — the lab site (`PhyEws`) and its report system (`wgyreport`) — has no TLS
  listener at all, which is not an oversight here: the original client used `http://` for every request
  to it, and the two systems' own pages are written against that origin. Android blocks cleartext by
  default from API 28 on, so without this the module fails before its first request with
  `CLEARTEXT communication to wlsy.xidian.edu.cn not permitted by network security policy` — the login
  never reaches the server, and the page reports a network failure that names nothing actionable.

  Deliberately **not** `android:usesCleartextTraffic="true"`: every other host this app talks to is HTTPS
  (IDS, eHall, the energy system, the campus card), and a blanket permission would silently permit plain
  HTTP to all of them if one ever changed its URL. The exemption is one host and its subdomains.

  What this costs is worth stating: the experiment account and password travel in the clear over the
  campus network, because that is the only way this system speaks. The password is still encrypted at
  rest (see `SecureStore`), and the original sent the same two fields over the same cleartext origin.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <domain-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <domain includeSubdomains="true">wlsy.xidian.edu.cn</domain>
    </domain-config>
</network-security-config>
